# Static bearer token checked by all /api/v1 route handlers.
# The server-side proxy at /api/proxy adds it to outgoing requests.
# The token never reaches the browser.
TM_API_TOKEN=tm-demo-4711

# Backend selection — the demo runs on the built-in mock by default.
# Unset (default): the mock route handlers in app/api/v1 serve /api/v1.
# Set to a real backend base URL (e.g. http://localhost:8000) and next.config.ts
# rewrites /api/v1/* to that backend instead (beforeFiles, so it wins over the
# filesystem mock handlers).
# TM_BE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Quix Portal API base URL. The topbar account menu fetches the signed-in
# user's profile (/profile) and organization (/organisations/current) from it,
# and `lib/portal/client.ts` derives the Portal origin for the postMessage
# handshake from it. This is completely separate from TM_API_TOKEN above.
#
# THE PLATFORM SETS THIS NAME. Every Quix deployment receives
# `Quix__Portal__Api` as a plain variable, so a deployment sets nothing by hand.
# `app/layout.tsx` reads it on the server and passes it to the browser through
# `PortalConfigProvider`. It carries NO `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix on purpose: that
# prefix makes `next build` inline the value, so one image would serve one
# environment only.
#
# It read `NEXT_PUBLIC_QUIX_PORTAL_API` until 19 Aug 2026, and that name
# defaulted to `https://portal-api.platform.quix.io`. No platform source names
# that host. The default is gone, so an absent value now fails loudly.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: leave it unset. There is no Portal in the local stack.
# The account menu then shows no profile and the Ask AI mode stays hidden.
# NEVER point a local stack at a real Portal host.
# Quix__Portal__Api=https://portal-api.dev.quix.io

# Separate mechanism: the server-side proxy at app/api/proxy reads its own
# API_URL variable (used by the contract guard) to pick its forwarding target.
# API_URL is independent of TM_BE_URL and unaffected by it — leave it unset
# for the demo.
# API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# THE QUIXLAB URL IS NOT SET HERE. It belongs to the API deployment.
#
# The API reads `TM_QUIXLAB_URL` and answers
# `GET /api/v1/integrations/quixlab-url`. `app/layout.tsx` asks that route on
# the server, through `lib/quixlab-server.ts`, and `QuixLabConfigProvider`
# passes the answer to the browser. So one operator sets one name on one
# deployment, and the two sides can never disagree.
#
# A configured URL shows the four "Open in QuixLab" controls. No URL hides them.
# This front end needs API_URL or TM_BE_URL, plus TM_API_TOKEN, to ask at all.

# THE FLIGHT TEST STATION URL IS NOT SET HERE EITHER, for the same reason.
#
# The API reads `TM_FTS_URL` and answers `GET /api/v1/integrations/fts-url`;
# `lib/fts-server.ts` asks it and `FtsConfigProvider` passes it down. It must
# be the station's PUBLIC url: the browser frames it, so an internal service
# name will not resolve.
